LLM in Cellular Network Domain. LLMs demonstrate strong performance on RANPerf, indicating that they can effectively adapt to certain downstream networking tasks without explicit domain knowledge. However, for the RAN tasks considered in this work, LLM-only adaptation is less effective for fine-grained quantitative modeling, likely because general-purpose LLM pretraining is not specifically optimized for precise numerical and temporal representations. RAN-NFM-Pro therefore combines RAN-specific representations learned from operational data with LLM-derived semantic and contextual features, allowing the two sources of representation to complement each other. This design also provides flexibility in deployment: Core favors efficiency, while Pro targets accuracy-first scenarios where additional computational cost is acceptable. As LLM capabilities continue to evolve, the optional LLM branch provides a natural interface for incorporating stronger LLM representations without redesigning the RAN-specific backbone.
